#### A practical look at what's genuinely backed by research for better sleep — melatonin dosing, magnesium, wind-down routines, and why snoring is worth taking seriously — plus a few things that work for me personally.

## Why sleep is worth taking seriously

Poor sleep isn't just about feeling tired. It touches nearly every system in the body — blood sugar regulation, immune function, mood, cardiovascular health, and how well your body handles stress hormones like cortisol, which matters even more if you're dealing with any kind of hormonal or metabolic issue on top of everyday life. And as the section below on snoring lays out, sleep quality and heart health are far more directly connected than most people realize. The CDC recommends 7 or more hours a night for adults 18-60, narrowing slightly to 7-9 hours for ages 61-64 and 7-8 for 65 and up — but the widely cited "7 to 9 hours" range for working-age adults comes from the American Academy of Sleep Medicine and Sleep Research Society's own joint consensus statement, which the CDC itself lists as a source. Either way, sleep physicians increasingly emphasize that a consistent wake-up time, not just a consistent bedtime, is the real anchor of a healthy sleep schedule. Pick your wake time first, then work backward.

## The basics that do the most work

None of this is flashy, but it's what actually moves the needle according to sleep researchers:

**Get real daylight in the morning.** The CDC recommends at least 30 minutes of natural light exposure during the day, ideally soon after waking. Morning light helps set your circadian clock, making it considerably easier to fall asleep at a reasonable hour that night.

**Keep the room cool and dark.** Sleep medicine specialists generally recommend a bedroom temperature between 65 and 68°F. Blackout curtains and a white-noise machine can help if street noise or light is an issue.

**Watch screens before bed.** Blue light from phones and tablets suppresses melatonin production and, in some studies, can delay its release by up to 2 hours, which pushes your whole sleep schedule later. The CDC's general guidance is to avoid screens for at least 30 minutes before bed; some researchers now think it's less about the blue light specifically and more about how stimulating the content is — a boring e-book at low brightness is a very different thing than scrolling an argument on social media right before lights-out.

**If you can't sleep, don't just lie there.** Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is recognized by the NIH as the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia — more effective long-term than sleep medication for most people. A core piece of it: if you're not asleep within about 20 minutes, get up, do something calm and boring in dim light, and go back to bed when you're actually sleepy. Lying in bed, frustrated, just teaches your brain to associate the bed with being awake.

## If you snore, this section is the one that actually matters most

Everything above is about optimizing decent sleep. This part is about something more urgent: loud, habitual snoring — especially snoring paired with gasping, choking sounds, long pauses in breathing that someone else has noticed, morning headaches, or daytime sleepiness so heavy it feels unshakeable — isn't just an annoyance to whoever's sleeping next to you. It can be a sign of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), a condition in which the airway repeatedly collapses or narrows during sleep, and it deserves proper medical attention, not a mental note to "get around to it eventually."

The reason to treat it with urgency is the cardiovascular connection, and it's a strong one. The American Heart Association has published a formal scientific statement on OSA and cardiovascular disease, and the association isn't subtle: OSA is strongly linked to high blood pressure, atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, and heart failure. Every pause in breathing during sleep is a small crisis for your body — oxygen drops, the heart works harder, stress hormones spike — and when that happens dozens or hundreds of times a night, for years, it adds up to measurable damage. A 2026 study out of Flinders University added an important wrinkle to this picture: it's not just how severe someone's sleep apnea is on average that predicts heart risk, but how much it swings from night to night — people with highly variable breathing problems night to night had roughly 30% higher odds of a heart attack, stroke, or heart failure than people with more consistent patterns. Untreated sleep apnea also interacts badly with everything else on this list: it wrecks the very sleep architecture that keeps blood sugar, mood, and immune function on track, so someone with untreated OSA is fighting a system-wide headwind, not just a snoring problem.

The good news is that this is genuinely treatable, and the path is well-established. If snoring is loud, frequent, or accompanied by any of the warning signs above, the right move is to see a sleep medicine specialist or pulmonologist, who can order a sleep study (in a lab or, increasingly, at home) to check for OSA. If snoring seems more mechanical — a stuffy nose, mouth breathing, a deviated septum, or enlarged tonsils — an ENT is often the right first stop instead. Not everyone who snores has sleep apnea, and not everyone who snores needs a CPAP machine; plenty of snoring resolves with weight management, positional changes, or treatment of nasal congestion. But when a sleep study confirms OSA, CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure) is the gold-standard treatment, and it works: it's effective across all severities of OSA and reliably eliminates breathing interruptions and restores normal oxygen levels during sleep for people who use it consistently. It's worth being honest that CPAP has a real adherence problem — a meaningful share of patients struggle with the mask, and many stop using it within the first few years, which is exactly why sticking with a sleep doctor for follow-up adjustments matters as much as the initial diagnosis. A CPAP machine gathering dust in a closet helps no one; getting the fit and settings right, with a doctor's help, is what makes it actually work.

If you've been told you snore, or someone in your house has mentioned it more than once, treat that as real information rather than background noise. Getting evaluated isn't an overreaction — it's a reasonable response to something with documented cardiovascular stakes.

## What the supplement research actually says

**Melatonin: less is genuinely more.** This one surprises people. The evidence-based sweet spot for melatonin is much lower than what most bottles on the shelf contain. Research consistently shows that low, physiological doses — roughly 0.3 to 1 milligram, taken 60 to 90 minutes before your target sleep time — perform as well as or better than the 5 or 10 milligram doses commonly sold in stores. One study found a 0.5 mg dose produced faster sleep onset than a 5 mg dose, likely because receptors get saturated at higher doses without any added benefit — you just get more grogginess and more vivid dreams the next morning. Most reviews put the practical ceiling around 3 mg for adults; older adults are generally advised to start even lower. If you've been taking 5 or 10 mg and still not sleeping well, it may genuinely be a case of less working better.

**Magnesium glycinate: a real but modest effect.** A 2025 randomized, placebo-controlled trial of 155 adults with poor sleep found that 250 mg of magnesium as bisglycinate produced a statistically significant, if modest, improvement in insomnia scores over four weeks. The effect size was small, and people with lower dietary magnesium intake to begin with saw the most benefit, which aligns with the idea that it helps more if you're correcting an actual shortfall rather than adding more on top of an already sufficient level. It's not a sedative, and it won't knock you out, but research supports it as a reasonable, low-risk component of a broader routine.

## What I personally do — with the honest caveats

I have my own nightly routine, and it's worth sharing what's evidence-based and what's more a matter of personal preference. I take 1 mg of [Natrol Fast Dissolve melatonin, which I reviewed here](https://v64otd.com/gear/natrol-melatonin-fast-dissolve-1mg-review/), along with [Double Wood Supplements' magnesium glycinate, also reviewed here](https://v64otd.com/gear/double-wood-magnesium-glycinate-400mg-review/), before bed — both doses that land squarely in the range the research above actually supports, for what that's worth.

I also built a Solfeggio-frequency playlist on Spotify that plays through the night to help calm my mind from excess cortisol. Here's the honest picture on that one: the scientific evidence specifically for "solfeggio frequencies" as a distinct phenomenon is thin. A small 2018 study found that five minutes of 528 Hz music lowered cortisol and raised oxytocin in participants, and a separate small study on 432 Hz found some calming effect on brainwave activity — but these are limited studies, not a settled body of research. What is well-established is that slow-tempo, calming music in general — classical, ambient, soft instrumental — reliably lowers anxiety, blood pressure, and heart rate, and supports better sleep. So whether it's the specific frequency doing the work or simply the fact that it's quiet, steady, non-lyrical music playing at low volume through the night, the practice itself aligns with what sleep researchers support. If it's working for you, there's no real downside to keeping it up.

## Putting together your own version

You don't need to copy anyone else's exact routine. A reasonable starting point, based on what the research actually supports: get outside for a bit soon after waking, keep your bedroom cool and dark, ease off screens (or at least stimulating content) in the last half hour before bed, and if you want to try a supplement, start low — 0.5 to 1 mg of melatonin rather than 10, and magnesium glycinate if you suspect your diet is light on magnesium. If none of that is enough and you're dealing with real, chronic insomnia, CBT-I is worth asking a doctor about before reaching for medication long-term.

And if a calming playlist helps you unwind, regardless of what you call the frequencies — there's real science behind slow, quiet music as a wind-down tool, even if the specific "solfeggio" branding is more marketing than proven mechanism.

